<header class="page-head">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h1>About the forum</h1>
    <p class="lede">A partnership of academics, researchers, state officers, community organisations and civil society organisations, formed in February 2018 to address the issues that keep small-scale fisheries from being sustainable.</p>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap split">
    <div>
      <h2>Why the forum was set up</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>“Small-scale fisheries make an important contribution to nutrition, food security, sustainable livelihoods and poverty alleviation, especially in developing countries. Despite this significant contribution, the issues constraining the sustainable development of small-scale fisheries remain poorly understood.” The FAO’s observation holds for Sri Lanka.</p>
      <p>When the forum was founded, about 570,000 people found direct or indirect work in fishing. The marine fishing population counted 190,000 households and 221,000 active fishers along the coast, with another 48,900 households and 54,450 fishers working lagoons and inland waters. Nearly all of them, and the people who process and market their catch, belong to the small-scale sector. Only the offshore and deep-sea multi-day fleet, owned mostly by non-fishing investors and producing for export, counts as large-scale.</p>
      <p>High rates of exploitation, unregulated technological change and rising fishing pressure are degrading aquatic ecosystems. State law has focused on the resource crisis, while fishers’ wellbeing has come second. Many fisheries co-operatives, once a working partnership between state and community, collapsed under changing political agendas. Climate change now adds sea level rise, lost landing and drying sites, and shifts in fish stocks.</p>
      <p>Sri Lanka has produced leading fisheries scientists, but research has concentrated on biology and oceanography rather than on fishing communities: their rights, their exclusion from decisions, and their declining livelihoods. The forum was created to fill that gap and to give fishers a voice in policy.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tide">
  <div class="wrap split">
    <div>
      <h2>What we set out to do</h2>
      <p class="muted">From the forum’s founding blueprint.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <ul>
        <li>Establish platforms to share information on small-scale fisheries.</li>
        <li>Voice the needs of small-scale fishing communities.</li>
        <li>Promote joint research on small-scale fisheries.</li>
        <li>Advise government on interventions for the sustainable development of small-scale fisheries, and provide policy inputs.</li>
        <li>Contribute to developing and implementing a national fisheries policy for Sri Lanka.</li>
        <li>Help bring the recommendations of the FAO Voluntary Guidelines into national legislation, policies and strategies, and into the programmes of civil society organisations.</li>
        <li>Build links with regional and international organisations working for sustainable small-scale fisheries, such as Too Big To Ignore and the International Collective in Support of Fishworkers.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap split">
    <div>
      <h2>How we work</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>We take a human rights-based approach. Small-scale fishing communities, women and men alike, should take part in the decisions that affect them and share responsibility for using fishery resources sustainably. We give particular weight to vulnerable and marginalised groups.</p>
      <p>Our main tool is the <strong>interactive platform</strong>: a meeting, held in a neutral setting such as a university, where fishers, government officers, scientists, the private sector and civil society deliberate on one issue and agree recommendations. Platforms help avoid duplicated effort, pool resources and put research findings in front of the people who make policy.</p>
      <p>Alongside these, members carry out research, write for the national press, and run workshops and projects directly with fishing communities.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tide">
  <div class="wrap split">
    <div>
      <h2>The SSF Guidelines</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The FAO Voluntary Guidelines for Securing Sustainable Small-Scale Fisheries in the Context of Food Security and Poverty Eradication recognise fishers’ rights and call for fisheries management that is participatory, inclusive and holistic, with special concern for women and marginalised people.</p>
      <p>Guidelines only work when governments adopt them. Since 2018 the forum has run sensitising workshops for state officers and consultations with fishing communities in the North, East, West and South to help Sri Lanka do so.</p>
      <p><a href="https://www.fao.org/voluntary-guidelines-small-scale-fisheries/en/" rel="noopener">Read the guidelines on the FAO website</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

